</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Difference by dataset</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that the average score for <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OAKS</span>-B (39.4%) is lower than that of <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OAKS</span>-N (57.5%).
We conjecture that the multiple-choice format of <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OAKS</span>-N introduces a restricted answer space,<span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">9</span>
              
              
              
            A random baseline can achieve an expected accuracy of 18.6%.</span></span></span> and that the narratives within <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OAKS</span>-N may still be susceptible to the influence of prior knowledge bias.
The lower performance on <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">OAKS</span>-B may be attributed to its synthetic design, which allows for the creation of intrinsically more complex and difficult tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
